__notes__.note.mass.what-nullity-fixes:
With the undetermined slot coefficient $X_{c}(x) = n_{0} + x + c\,Q(x)\,n_{\infty}$ one has $Q(X_{c}) = Q(x)(1 + 2c)$, so nullity of the point embedding for all $x$ fixes $c = -\tfrac12$. This is a statement about the coefficient, not about the chart's normalisation: rescaling the volume form $\varepsilon$ by $\kappa$ rescales $\ip{n_{0}}{n_{\infty}}$, $Q(x)$ and $Q(X_{c})$ alike, and nullity is insensitive to $\kappa$. What fixes the chart is the imported normalisation $\ip{n_{0}}{n_{\infty}} = +1$ together with $Q = Q(x)$ (Fact \claimref{mass.imported-flat-model-twistor-chart-infinity}); on the $x$-slot this reads $\ip{x}{y} = -\varepsilon_{ab}\varepsilon_{a'b'}x^{aa'}y^{bb'}$, so $Q(x) = -2\det(x^{aa'})$. The real points of the $x$-slot are, by definition, the $x$ whose translation generator $A(x\wedge n_{\infty})$ lies in $\mathfrak{su}(2,2)$ for the Hermitian form the relation displayed with \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism} fixed below (the reversion form $h$ on the conformal spinor space $\CS$, written in the chart's frame); by Lemma \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism}(2) that generator's block is $b_{x} = x\varepsilon$, and $x\varepsilon$ is anti-Hermitian iff $x = \tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon$ with $K$ Hermitian (if $x = \tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon$ then $x\varepsilon = -\tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K$; conversely $K := i\sqrt2\,x\varepsilon$ is Hermitian when $x\varepsilon$ is anti-Hermitian, and $x = -\tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon^{-1} = \tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon$). So the real points are the matrices
\begin{equation}
x^{aa'} = \frac{i}{\sqrt2}\,K\varepsilon, \qquad K = \begin{pmatrix} x^{0}+x^{3} & x^{1}-ix^{2} \\ x^{1}+ix^{2} & x^{0}-x^{3}\end{pmatrix},\quad \varepsilon = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 0\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
with $(x^{0}, x^{1}, x^{2}, x^{3})$ real, on which $Q = -2\det x = \det K = (x^{0})^{2} - (x^{1})^{2} - (x^{2})^{2} - (x^{3})^{2}$; the factor $i$ is that of the incidence relation (in geometric algebra the conformal pseudoscalar $I_6$, the complex unit of $\CS$, so that $x^{aa'}$ is $H_x$ up to that unit and the normalisation), and the factor $\varepsilon$ is what makes the translation generators of Lemma \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism} anti-Hermitian for the relation displayed with \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism} (Lemma \claimref{mass.grip-real}). The correspondence paper's reality structure \cite[Prop.~4.5]{ButlerT9} selects the same slice up to a choice of frame in $\Sc'$, which changes no formula of this section: the computations below are polynomial identities in the chart. The script checks these statements with the slice scaled by $\sqrt2$ (so that $Q = 2\det K$ there), the normalisation $1/\sqrt2$ being a real scalar that affects no reality statement; it also confirms that the anti-Hermiticity condition on the eight real parameters of $x$ has rank four, so that the slice is all of the real locus.

__notes__.note.mass.setting-grip-composite-frozen-:
The classical phase space of $N$ twistors carries the Hermitian form $\Sigma$ of signature $(2,2)$, written $\bar{Z}\cdot W := \Sigma(Z, W)$, and the conformal group acts by $SU(2,2)$. The form is canonical up to a phase and has signature $(2,2)$ \cite[Prop.~4.5(1), (2), (4)]{ButlerT9}; the chart frame fixes the phase, and in it $\Sigma$ is the standard twistor form $\bar{Z}\cdot Z = \omega^{A}\bar\pi_{A} + \bar\omega^{A'}\pi_{A'}$ \cite{PenroseRindler1986}, which pairs $\omega$ with $\bar\pi$ through the frame: for $Z = (\omega, \pi)$ and $W = (\xi, \chi)$,
\begin{equation}
\bar{Z}\cdot W = \hp{\pi}{\xi} + \overline{\hp{\chi}{\omega}}, \qquad \hp{u}{v} := \overline{u^{3}}\,v^{1} + \overline{u^{4}}\,v^{2}\quad (u \in \Sc',\ v \in \Sc),
\end{equation}
the sesquilinear pairing that identifies the frame $e_{3}, e_{4}$ of $\Sc'$ with the frame $e_{1}, e_{2}$ of $\Sc$; we write $\bar{u}_{a}$ for the conjugate of the component of $u \in \Sc'$ so identified with $e_{a}$, so that $\hp{u}{v} = \bar{u}_{a}v^{a}$. The real slice \claimref{note.mass.what-nullity-fixes} is the one this form determines. The moment map of the action on the phase space is the classical form of the generators of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-flat-model-twistor-chart-infinity}:
\begin{equation}
M_{A}(Z^{1}, \dots, Z^{N}) := I_{6}\sum_{i=1}^{N} \bar{Z}^{i}\cdot(A Z^{i}), \qquad A \in \mathfrak{sl}(\T),
\end{equation}
with $I_{6}$ the complex unit of the conformal spinor space (acting on conformal spinors by right multiplication; values of $\Sigma$ and components are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_{6}\}$, the bar reversing $I_{6}$), real for $A \in \mathfrak{su}(2,2)$, and $M_{U\wedge V} := M_{A(U\wedge V)}$ for ambient $U, V$. Equivariance is built in: for $\gamma \in SU(2,2)$, $M_{A}(\gamma Z) = M_{\gamma^{-1}A\gamma}(Z)$, and $A(\gamma U \wedge \gamma V) = \gamma\,A(U\wedge V)\,\gamma^{-1}$ because the isomorphism of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-flat-model-twistor-chart-infinity} intertwines the two actions.

correspondence.chiral-planes-summand-spa.formal:
Let $\mathfrak{l} := \Lambda^2 (W_{1,3})_\C \subset \mathfrak{so}(V_\C)$ be the complexified Lorentz algebra of the splitting, acting on $W_\C := (W_{1,3})_\C$ by the bivector action and on $\Som$, $\Spi$ through $\rho$, and let $\mathfrak{l} = \mathfrak{g}^+ \oplus \mathfrak{g}^-$ be its decomposition into two commuting subalgebras each isomorphic to $\mathfrak{sl}(2,\C)$ (Proposition \claimref{correspondence.chirality-split-ordering-insensitivity-asd-sector}(1): a statement about $\Lambda^2 W_{1,3}$ alone, whose proof uses nothing from this section). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the map $\Som \otimes \Spi \to W_\C$, $\omega\otimes\pi \mapsto \Psi^{-1}(\omega\wedge\pi)$, is an isomorphism of $\mathfrak{l}$-modules, and one of $\mathfrak{g}^\pm$ acts faithfully on $\Som$ and trivially on $\Spi$ while the other acts faithfully on $\Spi$ and trivially on $\Som$: the two planes are the two chiral doublets;
\item $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathfrak{l}}(W_\C \otimes \Spi,\ \Som)$ and $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathfrak{l}}(W_\C \otimes \Som,\ \Spi)$ are one-dimensional.
\end{enumerate}

correspondence.chirality-split-ordering-.formal:
Let $\mathfrak{l} := \Lambda^2 (W_{1,3})_\C \cong \mathfrak{so}(1,3)_\C \subset \mathfrak{so}(2,4)_\C$ be the complexified Lorentz subalgebra of the splitting, and $\mathfrak{l} = \mathfrak{g}^+ \oplus \mathfrak{g}^-$ its decomposition into the $\pm i$ eigenspaces of the duality operator of Lemma \claimref{correspondence.lorentzian-duality-complex-structure}. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item each of $\mathfrak{g}^\pm$ is a subalgebra isomorphic to $\mathfrak{sl}(2,\C)$, and $[\mathfrak{g}^+, \mathfrak{g}^-] = 0$: the self-dual and anti-self-dual sectors commute;
\item (\emph{ordering insensitivity of the ASD sector}) Let $\mathcal{A}^- \subset \mathcal{P}$ be the unital subalgebra generated by $q(\rho(\mathfrak{g}^-))$, and call $q(\rho(\mathfrak{g}^+))$ the \emph{googly slot}. Then: (a) every ordering discrepancy of a word in elements of $q(\rho(\mathfrak{g}^-))$ lies in $\hbar\,\mathcal{A}^-$, and for a word of length two it is exactly $\hbar\,q(\rho([P, Q])) \in \hbar\,q(\rho(\mathfrak{g}^-))$; (b) $\mathcal{A}^-$ commutes with the googly slot, $[\mathcal{A}^-, q(\rho(\mathfrak{g}^+))] = 0$; (c) under the adjoint action of $\mathfrak{g}^+$ through $q\circ\rho/\hbar$, $\mathcal{P}$ is a locally finite $\mathfrak{g}^+$-module, the googly slot is a copy of the adjoint representation inside it, and every element of $\mathcal{A}^-$ (in particular every discrepancy of (a)) has zero component in the adjoint isotypic subspace: reorderings within the anti-self-dual sector never source the googly slot, at quartic order or at any order.
\end{enumerate}

correspondence.incidence-relation-chart.technical:
Fix bases with $\pi_1\wedge\pi_2 = N_0$ in $\Spi$ and $e_1\wedge e_2 = N_\infty$ in $\Som$. For a linear map $A: \Spi	o\Som$ the graph line $L_A$ has Pl"ucker bivector $P(A) = (A\pi_1 + \pi_1)\wedge(A\pi_2 + \pi_2)$, which expands as $P(A) = N_0 + \varphi(A) + (\det A)\,N_\infty$ with $\varphi(A)$ in the mixed slot $\Som\wedge\Spi$. The map $\varphi$ is a linear isomorphism onto $\{
O,
Inf\}^\perp\otimes\C$ with $Q(\varphi(A)) = -2\det A$, so writing $m(A) = \varphi(A)$ one gets $P(A) = 
O + m(A) - 	frac12 Q(m(A))\,
Inf = X(m(A))$: the Pl"ucker bivector is exactly the completion's point embedding, complexified, and its nullity is the identity $\det A = -	frac12 Q(m(A))$.

The map $A \mapsto L_A$ is a bijection onto the lines of $\PCS$ skew to $L_\infty$, and $[P(A)]$ ranges exactly over the points of the Klein quadric with $\ip{P}{
Inf} 
eq 0$, the affine chart of the complexified conformal compactification. Consequently the adjoined null pair is the pair of skew lines $(L_\infty, L_0)$, the splitting of a conformal spinor into $(\omega, \pi)$ is the Witt completion at spinor level, the incidence relation (Penrose's $\omega = ix\pi$, in the real algebra $\omega = 	frac12 x\,
Inf\,\pi$) is the chart map at $A = I_6\hat x$ with $I_6$ the complex unit of $\CS$ and $\hat x$ Hermitian for the invariant form $\Sigma$, and the stabiliser of $L_\infty$ is the Poincar'e parabolic, whose Levi factor acts on $\Som$ and $\Spi$ as the two chiral doublets.

correspondence.one-statement-three-langu.formal:
Define the \emph{infinity bivector} of a scale $g$ (Penrose's infinity twistor) as $I_{\alpha\beta} := \Psi(I_g) \in \Lambda^2\CS$, a bivector of the conformal spinor space, with $I_g = (1,0,\rho_g)$ the scale tractor of Fact \claimref{correspondence.imported-vacuum-equations-scale-tractor}. Then, on the region of invertibility of a gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), with covariant constancy taken for the tractor gauge field of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection} and its action on $\CS$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item vacuum with $\Lambda = 0$ holds if and only if the infinity bivector is covariantly constant and \emph{simple} (a line of $\PCS$: the infinity line);
\item the Einstein condition with $\Lambda \neq 0$ holds if and only if the infinity bivector is covariantly constant with $\varepsilon(I\wedge I) = \Lambda/3 \neq 0$: a non-simple bivector, which is no line at all.
\end{enumerate}
Field equations, tractor parallelism, and infinity-bivector constancy are one statement in three languages, and the classical fact of twistor theory that the infinity bivector is simple precisely when $\Lambda = 0$ is the transport principle's nullity criterion (Theorem \claimref{correspondence.spinor-identification}(2)) applied to Fact \claimref{correspondence.imported-vacuum-equations-scale-tractor}.

correspondence.ordering-filtration.formal:
Grade $\mathcal{P}$ by total degree in the generators. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item any two orderings of a monomial of degree $\le 2$ differ by an element of $\hbar\,\C$: quadratic ordering ambiguities are central scalars and carry no tensorial information;
\item any two orderings of a monomial of degree $4$ differ by $\hbar\,(\text{a quadratic}) + \hbar^2\,\C$; for a \emph{balanced} monomial (two $Z$'s and two $\bar Z$'s) the quadratic is a mixed one, i.e.\ of the form $q(M) + \hbar\C$ with $M \in \mathfrak{gl}(\CS)$, and the ordering discrepancy of a product $q(M)q(N)$ of symmetrised quadratics taken in the two orders is $[q(M), q(N)] = \hbar\,q([M,N])$: balanced quartic ordering ambiguities are valued, through $q$, in $\mathfrak{gl}(\CS)$, with $\CS$ the conformal spinor space (twistor space), which contains the conformal algebra $\mathfrak{so}(2,4)_\C \cong \mathfrak{sl}(\CS)$;
\item quartic order is the \emph{first} at which an ordering ambiguity can be $\mathfrak{gl}(\CS)$-valued.
\end{enumerate}

correspondence.shared-core-ordering-dict.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The symmetrised quadratics of the palatial algebra close under commutator on a copy of $\mathfrak{gl}(4,\C) = \mathfrak{gl}(\CS)$, with $\CS$ the conformal spinor space (twistor space) (Lemma \claimref{correspondence.symmetrised-quadratics-palatial-algebra}); the anti-Hermitian part with respect to the Hermitian form $\Sigma$ of Proposition \claimref{correspondence.reality-conjugation-twistor-form-factor} is $\mathfrak{u}(2,2)$, containing $\mathfrak{su}(2,2) \cong \mathfrak{so}(2,4)$.
\item The bivectors of $\Cl(2,4)_\C$ close under commutator on $\mathfrak{so}(2,4)_\C$ and act on $\CS$ as $\mathfrak{sl}(\CS)$ (Proposition \claimref{correspondence.spin-representation}).
\item Under the Klein identification the two copies coincide, compatibly with their actions: the assignment $\beta \mapsto q(\rho(\beta))/\hbar$ is an injective Lie algebra homomorphism $\mathfrak{so}(2,4)_\C \to \mathcal{P}$, and the adjoint action of $q(\rho(\beta))/\hbar$ on the linear palatial generators $Z^\alpha$, $\bar Z_\beta$ is the spinor action $\rho(\beta)$ of the Clifford bivector, in the dual pair of slots of Lemma \claimref{correspondence.symmetrised-quadratics-palatial-algebra}(1) ($-\rho(\beta)$ on the $Z$ slot, its transpose on the $\bar Z$ slot).
\item Both quantisations incur the same ordering shift of one half per dimension: on the palatial storey, symmetric ordering shifts the distinguished $\mathfrak{u}(1)$ generator by $-\tfrac{\hbar}{2}$ per complex dimension of $\CS$, totalling $-2\hbar$ (Lemma \claimref{correspondence.symmetrised-quadratics-palatial-algebra}(3)); on the Clifford storey, the boost generator of each adjoined plane is $N - \tfrac12$: minus one half per fermionic mode, the same sign (Proposition \claimref{correspondence.clifford-side-ordering-shift}).
\end{enumerate}

correspondence.skew-lines-splittings-hyp.formal:
The following three data on the conformal spinor space $\CS$ (twistor space) determine one another naturally:
\begin{enumerate}
\item an ordered pair of skew (disjoint) lines $(L_\infty, L_0)$ in $\PCS$, with a joint normalisation of scale;
\item a direct-sum splitting $\CS = \Som \oplus \Spi$ into two $2$-planes, with volume elements $N_\infty \in \Lambda^2\Som$, $N_0 \in \Lambda^2\Spi$ normalised by $\varepsilon(N_0 \wedge N_\infty) = 1$;
\item a hyperbolic plane in $V_\C$ spanned by an ordered pair of null vectors $\nInf, \nO$ with $\ip{\nO}{\nInf} = 1$.
\end{enumerate}
Under the correspondence, the mixed subspace $\Som \wedge \Spi \subset \Lambda^2\CS$ is exactly the orthogonal complement $\{\nO, \nInf\}^\perp$, of dimension four.

