Three data on the conformal spinor space $\CS$ determine one another naturally: an ordered pair of skew lines $(L_\infty, L_0)$ in $\PCS$ with a joint normalisation of scale; a splitting $\CS = \Som\oplus\Spi$ into two planes with volume elements $N_\infty \in \Lambda^2\Som$, $N_0 \in \Lambda^2\Spi$ normalised by $\varepsilon(N_0\wedge N_\infty) = 1$; and a hyperbolic plane in $V_\C$ spanned by null vectors $
Inf$, $
O$ with $\ip{
O}{
Inf} = 1$. Under this correspondence the mixed subspace $\Som\wedge\Spi \subset \Lambda^2\CS$ is exactly the four-dimensional orthogonal complement $\{
O, 
Inf\}^\perp$.

proofs.main:
(1) $\leftrightarrow$ (2): a line of $\PCS$ is a $2$-plane of $\CS$; two lines are skew exactly when the planes meet in $0$, i.e.\ split $\CS$; volume elements of the planes are fixed up to scale, and $\varepsilon(N_0\wedge N_\infty) 
eq 0$ because the four spanning vectors form a basis, so the joint normalisation is available. (2) $\leftrightarrow$ (3): $N_\infty$ and $N_0$ are simple, hence null vectors of $V_\C$ (Theorem \claimref{correspondence.spinor-identification}(2)), with $\ip{
O}{
Inf} = \varepsilon(N_0\wedge N_\infty) = 1$; conversely a null pair with pairing $1$ gives simple bivectors whose planes are complementary (if the planes met, the wedge would vanish). Orthogonality of the mixed slot: a wedge of the form $N_0 \wedge (s\wedge p)$ with $s \in \Som$, $p \in \Spi$ contains three factors from the $2$-plane $\Spi$, hence vanishes; likewise for $N_\infty$. The mixed slot has dimension $2 \cdot 2 = 4$ and the complement has dimension $6 - 2 = 4$; since $\Lambda^2\CS = \Lambda^2\Som \oplus (\Som\wedge\Spi) \oplus \Lambda^2\Spi$ and the two extreme summands span a nondegenerate (hyperbolic) plane, the middle summand is the complement exactly.

correspondence.spinor-identification.formal:
With $\CS = \Lambda^{\mathrm{even}} W$ as in Theorem \claimref{correspondence.fock-model-complexified-ascended-algebra}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item there is a canonical isomorphism $V_\C \cong \Lambda^2 \CS$ of $\mathrm{Spin}$-modules, under which the quadratic form corresponds to $P \mapsto \varepsilon(P \wedge P)$ for a fixed volume functional $\varepsilon$ on $\Lambda^4\CS$;
\item a vector is null exactly when its bivector is simple (decomposable), that is, represents a 2-plane of $\CS$, that is, a line of the projective spinor space $\PCS := \mathbb{P}(\CS)$ (projective twistor space);
\item consequently the completion's fibre quadric (Fact \claimref{correspondence.imported-forced-completion-fibre-quadric}), the space of null rays of $V$, double covers the real slice of the Klein quadric (a ray and its opposite give one point), whose points are the null lines of $V$ and are lines of $\PCS$.
\end{enumerate}

correspondence.symmetrised-quadratics-pa.formal:
For $M \in \mathfrak{gl}(4,\C)$ with entries $M^\beta{}_\alpha$ set
\[
q(M) \;:=\; \tfrac12 \sum_{\alpha,\beta} M^\beta{}_\alpha\,\bigl(Z^\alpha \bar Z_\beta + \bar Z_\beta Z^\alpha\bigr) \;\in\; \mathcal{P}.
\]
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $[q(M), Z^\gamma] = -\hbar\,(MZ)^\gamma$ and $[q(M), \bar Z_\gamma] = +\hbar\,(M^{\mathsf T}\bar Z)_\gamma$: the adjoint action of $q(M)$ on the linear generators is the linear action of $M$ (in the dual pair of representations), times $\hbar$;
\item $[q(M), q(N)] = \hbar\, q([M,N])$: the map $M \mapsto q(M)/\hbar$ is an injective Lie algebra homomorphism $\mathfrak{gl}(4,\C) \to \mathcal{P}$;
\item $q(M) = \sum M^\beta{}_\alpha Z^\alpha \bar Z_\beta - \tfrac{\hbar}{2}\operatorname{tr} M$: the symmetric and normal orderings of a quadratic differ by the central element $\tfrac{\hbar}{2}\operatorname{tr}M$; for the distinguished generator $M = \mathrm{id}$ (the helicity/homogeneity direction) the shift is $-2\hbar$, that is $q(\mathrm{id}) = \sum_\alpha Z^\alpha\bar Z_\alpha - 2\hbar$: minus one half of $\hbar$ for each of the four complex dimensions of the conformal spinor space $\CS$ (twistor space).
\end{enumerate}

correspondence.twistors-are-states-three.formal:
Let $u \in \CS\setminus\{0\}$ and define its \emph{annihilator space} $W_u := \{v \in V_\C : c(v)u = 0\}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $W_u$ is a maximal totally isotropic ($3$-dimensional) subspace of $V_\C$: \emph{every} conformal spinor (twistor) is a pure spinor. Moreover $W_u$ determines $u$ up to scale (the joint kernel of $c(W_u)$ is the line $\C u$), so $[u] \mapsto W_u$ is an injection of $\PCS$ into the maximal isotropic subspaces, equivariant for the spin action.
\item For any maximal isotropic complement $U'$ dual to $W_u$, the map $\Lambda^\bullet U' \to \Lambda^\bullet W$, $\xi \mapsto c(\xi)u$, is a linear isomorphism: $u$ is the Fock vacuum of the three-ladder-pair system built on $(W_u, U')$, every state of the module is generated from $u$ by its own pair operators, and $\CS$ (respectively $\CSm$) is what the even (respectively odd) products generate. The pair system is canonical given $u$ up to the choice of dual complement.
\item Ambient vectors act on conformal spinor states as sums of three fermionic creation and annihilation operators; conformal spinors are therefore not \emph{operators} of the pair algebra but its \emph{states}. The annihilator spaces of $\CS$-spinors and of $\CSm$-spinors are disjoint families (no maximal isotropic annihilates both a nonzero even and a nonzero odd spinor).
\end{enumerate}

mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism.formal:
For $x = x^{aa'}e_{a}\wedge e_{a'}$ in the $x$-slot:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $A(n_{\infty}\wedge n_{0}) = \operatorname{diag}(-\tfrac12, -\tfrac12, \tfrac12, \tfrac12)$, the grading element;
\item $A(x \wedge n_{\infty}) = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & b_{x} \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ with $b_{x}: \Spi \to \Som$, $(b_{x}\pi)^{a} = x^{aa'}\varepsilon_{a'b'}\pi^{b'}$: the translations;
\item $A(x \wedge n_{0}) = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ c_{x} & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ with $c_{x}: \Som \to \Spi$, $(c_{x}\omega)^{a'} = -x^{ba'}\varepsilon_{ba}\omega^{a}$: the special conformal generators.
\end{enumerate}

odd-extensions.boost-dilation-ratio-gaug.formal:
In the rung, with the canonical null frame realised as $I = (e_{+} + e_{-})/2$, $\varphi = e_{+} - e_{-}$ in the hyperbolic plane orthogonal to the Lorentz frame:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the bivector $B = I \wedge \varphi = -e_{+}e_{-}$ squares to $+1$, commutes with every Lorentz bivector and with every Lorentz vector, and anticommutes with $e_{\pm}$: it is the generator of the dilations of the conformal model;
\item[(ii)] the rotor $R = \cosh\theta + B\sinh\theta$ fixes the Lorentz frame and acts on the plane by $I \mapsto tI$, $\varphi \mapsto \varphi/t$ with $t = e^{2\theta}$, so the boosts of Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.transport-constrains-frozen-point-only-through}(iii) are the dilations;
\item[(iii)] hence the ratio $b/a$ of the frozen point's components is a dilation gauge and only the product $2ab = \Lambda/3$ is invariant, and by Fact \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation} the gauge is not fixed by the dynamics either: the components are unphysical, not merely symmetry-related, and the frame the transport supplies carries no quantity beyond $\Lambda$ itself.
\end{enumerate}

odd-extensions.twistor-algebra.formal:
Let $\Cl(2,4)$ be the ambient of this world's conformal completion (Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-conformal-completion}). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
